---
import ServicePage from '../components/ServicePage.astro';
---

<ServicePage
  slug="retention-expansion"
  title="Retention & Expansion: Keep Customers and Grow Their Value"
  description="Retention, expansion, and reactivation systems that grow revenue from customers you already have. Health scoring for SaaS; repeat business for local."
  serviceName="Retention & Expansion"
  serviceTag="Retention & Expansion"
  serviceSchemaDescription="Retention, expansion, and reactivation programs: lifecycle campaigns, churn and health scoring for SaaS, and repeat-business, reviews, and reactivation for local businesses."
  headlineParts={['Keep your customers.', 'And grow what they spend.']}
  subtitle="The cheapest revenue you will ever earn comes from customers you already have. I build the systems that keep them and grow them."
  hook="Acquisition gets all the attention, but most of the margin lives downstream. A customer who comes back, renews, refers, or buys more is worth far more than the cost of winning a new one. Yet retention is usually nobody's job: there is no follow-up, no reason to return, and no early warning before a customer quietly leaves. Fixing that is some of the highest-return work a growing business can do."
  whatsIncluded={[
    'Lifecycle and follow-up campaigns that bring customers back without manual effort',
    'For SaaS: health scoring and churn signals so at-risk accounts surface before they cancel',
    'For local: repeat-business, review, and win-back flows that drive return visits and referrals',
    'Expansion and renewal playbooks so growing an account is a process, not a hope',
  ]}
  goodFitIf={[
    'You win customers but too many of them quietly disappear after the first purchase',
    'Renewals and follow-up are manual, ad hoc, or simply not happening',
    'You have a base of past customers you have never systematically brought back',
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'I run a local business, not SaaS. Does this still apply?',
      htmlA: 'Yes. The mechanics differ but the principle is identical: it is cheaper to bring a customer back than to find a new one. For local businesses that means repeat-visit offers, review requests at the right moment, and reactivation campaigns for customers who have not returned. For SaaS it means renewals, health scoring, and expansion. I tailor the system to your model.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What is churn or health scoring?',
      htmlA: 'A health score combines the signals that predict whether a customer is likely to stay or leave (usage, engagement, support activity, payment behavior) into a single read your team can act on. It turns churn from a surprise into something you can see coming and intervene on.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do you run the program or just set it up?',
      htmlA: 'Either. I can build the system and hand it off documented, or own it on an ongoing basis as part of a retainer. The goal is always a motion that runs on systems, not on someone remembering to send the email.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How much does retention work cost?',
      htmlA: 'Pricing depends on scope. Standing up a retention or reactivation motion runs as a fixed-scope project. Ongoing ownership runs as a retainer. See the <a href="/pricing" class="link-teal">engagement models</a>, then book a call so we can share a precise number against your situation.',
    },
  ]}
  relatedServices={[
    { slug: 'crm-implementation', title: 'CRM Implementation', sub: 'The system that tracks every customer' },
    { slug: 'reporting-analytics', title: 'Reporting & Analytics', sub: 'See retention, churn, and lifetime value' },
    { slug: 'process-automation', title: 'Workflows & Automation', sub: 'Run the follow-up automatically' },
  ]}
  ctaHeadline="Ready to grow the revenue you already have?"
  ctaBody="Book a free ROI call. No pitch, no proposal. An honest look at where customers are slipping away and what would keep them."
  heroImage="/images/hero-crm-implementation.jpg"
/>
